    <comment>New package: qmqtt 1.0.7 - MQTT client library for Qt (Qt5 + Qt6)

QMQTT (https://github.com/emqx/qmqtt) is an MQTT 3.1/3.1.1 client library for
the Qt framework: plain TCP, TLS and WebSocket transports plus a topic router
that dispatches incoming messages by topic pattern. Version 1.0.7 is the
current release, tagged and published 2026-07-19, and is also the head of
upstream's master branch. Nothing named qmqtt exists in OBS today.

This supersedes SR#1373861, which briefly offered a Qt6-only build. Both Qt
majors are wanted, so this is back to a qt5/qt6 multibuild, laid out the way
Factory's qcustomplot already does it: source packages qmqtt-qt5 and
qmqtt-qt6, libraries libqmqtt-qt5-1 and libqmqtt-qt6-1, headers in
qmqtt-qt5-devel and qmqtt-qt6-devel. The two flavours share no installed path
- SONAMEs libqmqtt-qt5.so.1 and libqmqtt-qt6.so.1, headers under
/usr/include/qmqtt-qt5 and /usr/include/qmqtt-qt6, CMake packages qmqtt-qt5
and qmqtt-qt6 - so unlike qcustomplot they need no Conflicts and install side
by side.

jaero is submitted separately and builds against the qt5 flavour.

Licence: EPL-1.0 OR BSD-3-Clause, with the reasoning recorded in a
Legal-Review-Notice comment above the tag so it does not have to be
re-derived. In short: upstream's LICENSE offers the library under the Eclipse
Public License 1.0 *or* the Eclipse Distribution License 1.0, and the shipped
edl-v10 is the verbatim three-clause BSD template naming the Eclipse
Foundation - SPDX has no EDL-1.0 identifier and matches that text to
BSD-3-Clause. Every source file compiled into the library carries an in-file
BSD-3-Clause notice and nothing else, no file carries an EPL notice, and
README.md itself says &quot;New BSD License&quot;. EPL-1.0 being GPL-incompatible, the
GPL-3.0-or-later consumer jaero relies on the BSD-3-Clause arm, which the
&quot;OR&quot; grants. tests/gtest/gtest/ is a bundled googletest (also BSD-3-Clause)
that is built for the test suite and never installed.

Four patches. Three are upstream, one is deliberately ours:

 - qmqtt-tests-follow-ssl-option.patch, gh#emqx/qmqtt#269 - upstream compiles
   the test binary with QT_NO_SSL while the library defaults to SSL on.
   QT_NO_SSL removes pure virtuals from the middle of NetworkInterface and
   SocketInterface, so the mocks end up with a different vtable layout than
   the library they link, dispatch lands on a slot that is not there, and the
   suite dies with SIGSEGV.

 - qmqtt-drop-duplicate-cmake-export.patch, gh#emqx/qmqtt#271 - upstream
   installs the same CMake export twice, the second time under a name nothing
   includes.

 - qmqtt-tests-any-qt-major.patch, gh#emqx/qmqtt#270 - the CMake test tree
   hardcodes Qt5 and neither configures nor compiles against Qt6: the Qt Test
   component and the linked targets, the global endl manipulator Qt6 removed,
   missing &lt;climits&gt;/&lt;QIODevice&gt; includes, and
   QCoreApplication::hasPendingEvents which Qt6 also removed. It also stops
   adding tests/auto, which holds only the qmake module test and cannot pass
   in a CMake build of either Qt major.

 - qmqtt-flavoured-install-names.patch - PATCH-FIX-OPENSUSE. Adds a
   QMQTT_PACKAGE_NAME knob so the installed library and its CMake package can
   be renamed, which is what stops the two flavours claiming the same files
   and the same SONAME for two incompatible ABIs. Kept downstream on purpose:
   it exists to serve this package's flavour split.

Two build flags worth explaining:

 - -DQT_VERSION_MAJOR, for both flavours. Upstream reads QT_VERSION_MAJOR as
   an ordinary variable, but Qt5 never defines it and Qt6Config only
   publishes it as a directory property via set_directory_properties(). Its
   WebSockets branch therefore expands to find_package(Qt COMPONENTS
   WebSockets), lands in CMake's own FindQt module and dies looking for Qt3 -
   on either Qt major.

 - -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF, which reaches only the bundled googletest; the
   library gets its type from qmqtt_SHARED, which passes SHARED to
   add_library() explicitly. A shared googletest needs its consumers to
   define GTEST_LINKED_AS_SHARED_LIBRARY=1, which upstream's test target does
   not, so its global state is duplicated and the suite aborts at teardown
   with a double free.

Each flavour also refuses the other Qt major outright, so neither a stray Qt6
development package nor a missing one can quietly change which Qt a flavour
is built against.

%check runs the bundled Google Test suite for both flavours: 107 cases pass
under Qt5 and the same 107 under Qt6. Three are skipped by name via
GTEST_FILTER, with the reason in the spec - they assert behaviour the library
deliberately moved away from (isConnectedToHost now answers from
ClientPrivate's own connection state, and the reconnect timer moved from
onSocketError to onDisconnected) and were never updated. Upstream does not
notice because the only workflow in the repository is CodeQL.

Both flavours built locally on aarch64: green, no findings from the shared
library policy check, rpmlint clean apart from the invalid-spec-name every
multibuild source package emits.</comment>
